  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 142 PM CST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nOccasionally gusty southwest winds this afternoon have allowed\ntemperatures to warm up more than expected. Enjoy, because a\ncold front will pass this evening, and temperatures will drop.\nClouds and cold advection will keep temperatures in the 50s\nFriday, then as surface high pressure settles overhead, clear\nand calm conditions will allow temperatures to plunge into the\n30s areawide Friday night/Saturday morning. Highs in the 50s and\nlows in the 30s will be the norm through the weekend according \nto this NBM forecast. \n\nThe main concern over the weekend will be frost and freeze\npotential each night. Calm winds are expected over the Ozark\nFoothills and much of southern Illinois Friday night and that\nwill lead to the potential for temperatures to fall below\nfreezing and possibly even into the upper 20s in some valleys.\nElsewhere across the Quad State, temperatures should drop to 35\ndegrees or less and frost formation is likely. Saturday\nnight/Sunday morning will be the coldest night throughout the\nregion with most locations dropping to or just below the\nfreezing mark and widespread frost formation expected. Dewpoints\ncome up into the middle 30s for Sunday night, so while\ntemperatures may drop into the middle 30s with some frost\npotential, a freeze is not expected.\n\nAs for confidence levels and potential headlines, the NBM seems\nto be well to the cold side of guidance, and has 30-90% chances\nof reaching 32 degrees or below Friday night in southeast \nMissouri and southern Illinois, with similar chances throughout \nthe Quad State Saturday night. The NBM has a less than 10% \nchance of a hard freeze Saturday night, and no chance otherwise.\nAt this time, frost seems to be the more confident and \nwidespread concern, so would be leaning toward a Frost Advisory \nFriday night and Saturday night throughout the region to be\nissued either tonight or Friday morning. However, if \ntemperature guidance trends even lower, a Freeze Warning may be\nwarranted.\n\nTemperatures are expected to warm up some next week, as heights\naloft rise. This will lead to high temperatures climbing into\nthe 60s possibly Sunday and then into next week, but low\ntemperatures are expected to remain in the mid to upper 30s.",
